## Founder Persona Response
Move 1 — Niche down. Every failed startup tries to serve everyone. Don't say "I want to be a developer." Say "I'm learning React and I want to build dashboards for small businesses." That sentence gets you into conversations. "I want to be a developer" gets you ignored.
Move 2 — Ship your MVP. Founders don't wait until the product is perfect. Neither should you. A live project on a real domain, even if it's rough, signals that you finish things. That is incredibly rare and incredibly valuable.
Move 3 — Build in public. This is your marketing. Post your wins and your struggles on LinkedIn every week. "Day 14 — I finally understood CSS flexbox after 3 days of confusion." That post will get more engagement than a certificate ever will. Founders and hiring managers follow people who show their journey.
Move 4 — Warm leads only. Job boards are where your application goes to die among 400 others. Instead, find 5 founders or senior devs on LinkedIn every week. Engage with their posts genuinely. Then DM them: "I'm learning React and I've been following your product. I'd love feedback on this project I built." That's how you get noticed.
Move 5 — Interview as a problem-solver. Startups don't give you a textbook and ask you to memorise it. They give you a messy problem and watch how you think. Practice saying "I don't know, but here's how I'd figure it out" — that answer, delivered confidently, beats a wrong answer delivered with false confidence every time.
The hard truth: at 1–2 hours a day, you won't out-skill people with more time. So you need to out-hustle and out-position them. That's a game you can actually win.
